\title{Paradoxes as Theorems:\\A Finite Non-Explosive Calculus for Self-Negating Sentences}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We exhibit a finite paraconsistent calculus in which three pairwise distinct sentences, representing the common self-negating core of the Liar, Russell, and Berry paradoxes, are derivable gluts while an explicit false sentence remains underivable. The semantics is the four-valued support algebra $\{0,1\}^2$: one coordinate records positive support and the other negative support. Negation exchanges coordinates, designation requires positive support, and $(1,1)$ is a designated negation fixed point. A fixed point of complementation collapses every Boolean algebra; point-surjective diagonal self-reference therefore collapses Boolean semantics when applied to complementation. By contrast, our seven-sentence language has a negation-coherent valuation and an inductively generated derivability relation closed under double-negation introduction. Structural induction establishes soundness for derivations of arbitrary depth. It follows that an explicit false witness is not derivable, so explosion fails despite derivability of each paradox and its negation. A distinguished certificate is derivable and expresses the finite soundness property established by the metatheorem. This is finite reflection, not a claim that a strong arithmetical theory proves its own classical consistency.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The familiar response to semantic and set-theoretic paradox is restriction. The Liar motivates hierarchies or partial truth predicates; Russell's paradox motivates restricted comprehension; Berry's paradox exposes the instability of unrestricted definability in natural language. Those responses are indispensable when one wants a classical theory. A different question is nevertheless coherent: can the contradictory core of several paradoxes be admitted as theoremhood without making every sentence a theorem?

The answer depends on separating \emph{consistency} from \emph{nontriviality}. Classical consistency prohibits simultaneous derivations of $s$ and $\neg s$. Nontriviality asks only that some sentence remain underivable. In an explosive logic inconsistency implies triviality; in a paraconsistent logic it need not. We give an explicit finite witness: three distinct self-negating sentences are theorems and semantic gluts, but a control sentence is not a theorem.

The construction has two parts. First, ordinary Boolean semantics cannot house a self-negating value without collapse. A diagonal fixed-point principle connects expressive self-reference to this algebraic obstruction. Second, positive and negative support are represented by independent bits. Negation exchanges them rather than taking Boolean complement. The ``both'' value is then fixed by negation while remaining distinct from the other values.

Our language contains seven sentence codes. Three are abstract paradox names. Four controls realize true-only, false-only, neither, and a finite soundness certificate. The derivability relation has five axioms and closure under double-negation introduction. Derivation trees have unbounded depth even though syntax and valuation are finite, so soundness is proved structurally rather than by listing proofs.

The scope is exact. The paradox names model the common support profile relevant to self-negation; they do not encode quotation, bounded descriptions, or unrestricted comprehension. The certificate is an interpreted atom whose intended property is established externally. Neither choice amounts to a full compositional theory of truth or an evasion of incompleteness phenomena.

\section{Boolean semantics and diagonal collapse}

\begin{definition}[Boolean semantic algebra]
A Boolean algebra is a bounded distributive lattice $D$ with bottom $\bot$, top $\top$, meet $\wedge$, join $\vee$, and complementation $x\mapsto x^{\mathsf c}$ satisfying
\[
x\wedge x^{\mathsf c}=\bot,
\qquad
x\vee x^{\mathsf c}=\top.
\]
It is \emph{nontrivial} when $\bot\ne\top$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Boolean fixed-point collapse]
Let $D$ be a Boolean algebra. If $x\in D$ satisfies $x^{\mathsf c}=x$, then $\bot=\top$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The complement laws and the fixed-point equation give
\[
x\wedge x=x\wedge x^{\mathsf c}=\bot,
\qquad
x\vee x=x\vee x^{\mathsf c}=\top.
\]
Idempotence gives $x=\bot$ and $x=\top$, hence $\bot=\top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No nontrivial Boolean negation fixed point]
If $D$ is a nontrivial Boolean algebra, then $x^{\mathsf c}\ne x$ for every $x\in D$.
\end{corollary}

To connect the obstruction with diagonalization, take a code set $A$, a value space $D$, and an encoding $e:A\to(A\to D)$. Call $e$ \emph{point-surjective} when every function $f:A\to D$ equals $e(a)$ for some $a$. For any $g:D\to D$, define $d(a)=g(e(a)(a))$. Choose $a_0$ with $e(a_0)=d$ and put $b=e(a_0)(a_0)$. Then
\[
b=e(a_0)(a_0)=d(a_0)=g(e(a_0)(a_0))=g(b).
\]
Thus every endomorphism $g$ has a fixed point.

\begin{theorem}[Diagonal Boolean collapse]
Let $D$ be a Boolean algebra and $e:A\to(A\to D)$ be point-surjective. Then $\bot=\top$ in $D$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the diagonal argument to $g(b)=b^{\mathsf c}$. It yields $b=b^{\mathsf c}$, and the Boolean fixed-point collapse theorem applies.
\end{proof}

The problem is therefore not self-reference alone. It is the conjunction of sufficiently expressive diagonalization, Boolean complementation, and nontriviality. We retain self-negating values and nontriviality while changing the semantics of negation.

\section{Four-valued support semantics}

\begin{definition}[Support values]
A support value is a pair
\[
v=(p,n)\in\{0,1\}^2,
\]
where $p$ records positive support for a sentence and $n$ records positive support for its negation. Write
\[
\mathbf T=(1,0),\qquad
\mathbf F=(0,1),\qquad
\mathbf B=(1,1),\qquad
\mathbf N=(0,0),
\]
read as true only, false only, both, and neither.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Negation, designation, and glut]
Negation exchanges coordinates:
\[
\sim(p,n)=(n,p).
\]
A value $(p,n)$ is \emph{designated} exactly when $p=1$. It is a \emph{glut} exactly when $p=n=1$.
\end{definition}

The designated values are $\mathbf T$ and $\mathbf B$. Designation captures positive support, not exclusive truth.

\begin{lemma}[Involutive negation]
For every support value $v$, $\sim\!\sim v=v$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Swapping two coordinates twice restores their order.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Designated fixed point]
The value $\mathbf B$ is designated and $\sim\mathbf B=\mathbf B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Its positive coordinate is $1$, and exchanging the coordinates of $(1,1)$ changes nothing.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Characterization of gluts]
A support value is a glut if and only if it equals $\mathbf B$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The equations $p=1$ and $n=1$ uniquely determine $(1,1)$.
\end{proof}

Coordinate-swapping negation has two fixed points, $\mathbf B$ and $\mathbf N$, without collapse. The first is designated and contradictory; the second is undesignated and informationally incomplete.

\section{A seven-sentence language}

\begin{definition}[Sentence space]
Let
\[
S=\{L,R,B,T,F,G,C\}
\]
be seven pairwise distinct sentence codes. The first three name abstract Liar, Russell, and Berry sentences; $T$ is an ordinary truth, $F$ a false witness, $G$ a gap witness, and $C$ a finite soundness certificate. The Berry code $B$ is distinct from the bold semantic value $\mathbf B$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Syntactic negation]
Define $\nu:S\to S$ by
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\nu(L)=L, &&\nu(R)=R, &&\nu(B)=B,\\
&\nu(T)=F, &&\nu(F)=T, &&\nu(G)=G, &&\nu(C)=C.
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Valuation]
Define $v:S\to\{\mathbf T,\mathbf F,\mathbf B,\mathbf N\}$ by
\[
\begin{aligned}
&v(L)=v(R)=v(B)=\mathbf B,\\
&v(T)=\mathbf T,\quad v(F)=\mathbf F,\quad
v(G)=\mathbf N,\quad v(C)=\mathbf B.
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Negation coherence]
For every $s\in S$, $v(\nu(s))=\sim v(s)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $L,R,B,C$, both sides are $\mathbf B$; for $G$, both are $\mathbf N$. On $T,F$, syntactic negation swaps the codes while semantic negation swaps $\mathbf T$ and $\mathbf F$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Involutive syntactic negation]
For every $s\in S$, $\nu(\nu(s))=s$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Five codes are fixed by $\nu$, while $T$ and $F$ are exchanged.
\end{proof}

The controls separate semantic roles. The non-designated value of $F$ will witness nontriviality; $G$ shows that gaps coexist with gluts; and $T$ preserves ordinary unopposed positive support.

\section{Derivations and soundness}

\begin{definition}[Finite paradox calculus]
The relation $\vdash s$ is the least relation generated by
\[
\vdash L,\qquad \vdash R,\qquad \vdash B,\qquad
\vdash T,\qquad \vdash C,
\]
and the rule
\[
\frac{\vdash s}{\vdash\nu(\nu(s))}.
\]
A derivation is a finite tree built from these clauses. The rule can be repeated, so no fixed depth bounds all derivation trees.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness of derivations]
For every $s\in S$, if $\vdash s$, then $v(s)$ is designated.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use structural induction on a derivation. Each axiom is designated: the paradoxes and certificate have value $\mathbf B$, while $T$ has value $\mathbf T$. In the rule case, the conclusion $\nu(\nu(s))$ equals $s$ by involutivity and hence retains the designated value supplied by the induction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

The argument is proof-theoretic rather than merely enumerative. Repeated double negation creates infinitely many derivation trees, and induction establishes one invariant over their formation.

\begin{theorem}[Underivability of the false witness]
The sentence $F$ is not derivable: $\nvdash F$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\vdash F$, soundness would make $v(F)$ designated. But $v(F)=\mathbf F=(0,1)$ has positive coordinate $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nontriviality]
At least one sentence is not derivable, so the calculus is nontrivial.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take the sentence $F$.
\end{proof}

Nontriviality is the relevant replacement for classical consistency. The calculus contains semantic gluts but still distinguishes theorem from non-theorem.

\section{Three paradoxes and failure of explosion}

\begin{theorem}[Three distinct theorem gluts]
The codes $L$, $R$, and $B$ are pairwise distinct. Each is derivable, and each has glut value $\mathbf B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Distinctness is part of the seven-element construction. Derivability follows from the three paradox axioms. Their assigned value is $\mathbf B$, which is exactly the unique glut.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit failure of explosion]
There are sentences $p,q$ such that $p$ and its negation are derivable while $q$ is not. Specifically,
\[
\vdash L,\qquad \vdash\nu(L),\qquad \nvdash F.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Liar axiom gives $\vdash L$. Since $\nu(L)=L$, it also gives $\vdash\nu(L)$. The false-witness theorem gives $\nvdash F$.
\end{proof}

This is a syntactic counterexample to explosion, not merely an observation that four semantic values exist. A contradiction is derivable on both sides, while a named conclusion remains outside the derivability relation. Added inference rules could alter this property; the theorem concerns exactly the specified calculus.

\section{Finite reflection}

\begin{definition}[Expression of finite soundness]
A sentence $s\in S$ \emph{expresses finite soundness} when (i) $s=C$, and (ii) for every $q\in S$, if $\vdash q$, then $v(q)$ is designated.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Finite self-soundness]
The certificate $C$ is derivable, its value is designated, and it expresses finite soundness.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The certificate axiom gives $\vdash C$. Its value is $\mathbf B$, hence designated. It is the distinguished certificate by identity, and the universal designation clause is the soundness theorem.
\end{proof}

This result combines an object-level theorem with a metatheoretic interpretation. It is not a claim about an arithmetically strong theory proving its own classical consistency. There is no internal arithmetization of syntax, proof, or satisfaction. The certificate is an atom interpreted by a finite external definition. The theorem establishes finite reflection: the calculus proves its certificate, and the certificate's intended finite soundness condition is independently established.

That $v(C)=\mathbf B$ is instructive. Positive designation remains compatible with negative support; a certificate need not be true only in order to be assertible.

\section{The boundary theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Classical boundary and paraconsistent realization]
The following hold simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item for every nontrivial Boolean algebra $D$ and every $x\in D$, $x^{\mathsf c}\ne x$;
\item $L$, $R$, and $B$ are derivable;
\item every derivable sentence has a designated four-valued interpretation; and
\item $F$ is not derivable.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Clause (i) is the no-fixed-point corollary. Clause (ii) follows from the paradox axioms, clause (iii) is soundness, and clause (iv) is underivability of the false witness.
\end{proof}

A nontrivial Boolean semantics refuses self-negating values. Support-pair semantics accepts them by changing the operation assigned to negation, separating designation from exclusivity and soundness from classical consistency.

\section{Algorithms and finite audits}

\subsection{Support evaluation}
Represent a value as two bits. Negation is constant-time coordinate exchange; designation tests the first coordinate; glut detection tests both. Auditing $n$ sentence codes takes $O(n)$ time and constant auxiliary space beyond output.

\subsection{Derivability closure}
Initialize $A_0=\{L,R,B,T,C\}$. Repeatedly add $\nu(\nu(s))$ for each known theorem until no change occurs. Here double negation is identity, so one pass reaches the fixed point $A_0$. In a general finite language of $n$ codes, a worklist visits each newly derived code once and uses $O(n)$ expected set operations under constant-time hashing.

\subsection{Soundness and explosion audit}
Compute the closure, check positive support for each theorem, and search for a derived $s$ whose negation is also derived together with an underived $q$. The model returns $s=L$ and $q=F$. The scan is $O(n)$ after closure. These procedures illustrate the theory but do not replace the structural proofs.

\section{Applications}

In an inconsistent database, independent positive and negative reports naturally set separate bits. A conflict yields $\mathbf B$ rather than arbitrary consequences. Query systems can return records with positive support while flagging glut status.

Distributed replicas also benefit from four support profiles: agreement on truth, agreement on falsity, conflict, and absent information. The failure-of-explosion theorem models an operational requirement that one conflicting key not authorize unrelated facts.

Scientific, legal, and intelligence workflows often preserve contradictory testimony. A support layer offers a middle path between premature forced choice and uncontrolled propagation. Real systems would additionally require provenance, confidence, richer connectives, and revision policies.

For semantic paradox, the Boolean collapse theorem is diagnostic. If diagonalization generates a negation fixed point, Boolean complement semantics collapses. A four-valued fixed point can instead remain a stable glut. This identifies an algebraic resource for richer compositional truth theories without itself constructing one.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The three paradox names are abstract constants. The calculus does not encode quotation and substitution for the Liar, unrestricted comprehension for Russell, or descriptions and length for Berry. It proves coexistence for three distinct self-negating theorem codes.

The language has no conjunction, disjunction, implication, quantifiers, or comprehension principles. It therefore isolates non-explosion rather than supplying a general-purpose logic. Moreover, finite self-soundness is limited reflection: the certificate's interpretation incorporates the externally established condition, and no derivability predicate is arithmetized.

A first extension should construct a recursive language with quotation, substitution, a truth predicate, bounded description, and restricted comprehension. A monotone semantic operator in an information order may admit a least fixed point containing genuine diagonal Liar, Berry, and Russell gluts while preserving a non-designated arithmetic fragment.

A second problem is conservativity. If a paraconsistent theory extends a classical base with truth and restricted comprehension, every theorem in the truth-free, comprehension-free fragment should ideally already follow in the classical base. This would sharpen finite non-explosion into isolation of inconsistency from ordinary mathematics.

A third direction seeks internal soundness in an effective infinite system: a recursively axiomatized paraconsistent theory that proves a formula expressing designation of its derivations, remains nontrivial, and does not prove every sentence of its classical arithmetic fragment.

Finally, for $n$ pairwise distinct self-negating theorem codes and one non-designated code, at least $n+1$ codes appear necessary and should suffice. Requiring separate true-only and gap witnesses suggests a sharp bound of $n+3$. Such minimality theorems would generalize the present finite construction.

\section{Conclusion}

A contradiction becomes catastrophic only when the inference system grants it catastrophic reach. Boolean semantics explains why self-negation cannot be ordinary complement in a nontrivial truth algebra. Four-valued support semantics changes the geometry: positive and negative evidence occupy independent coordinates, and $\mathbf B$ is a designated negation fixed point.

In the seven-sentence calculus, the Liar, Russell, and Berry codes are pairwise distinct derivable gluts. Every derivation is semantically designated. The explicit false witness remains underivable, directly refuting explosion. A distinguished theorem also serves as a finite soundness certificate under the stated interpretation. The result is not elimination of paradox but localization: self-negating sentences can be theorems, contradictions can be recorded honestly, and the theory can still say no.

