# An Interactive Tour of Contradiction Without Collapse

A classical truth value behaves like a switch; negation flips it. A support value behaves like two independent indicators, one for evidence in favor and one for evidence against. Begin with the interactive support explorer.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The four states are closely related to [four-valued logic](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/logic-paraconsistent/) and the broader idea of [paraconsistent reasoning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paraconsistent_logic). The geometric plot makes the two-coordinate structure visible.

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the Boolean fixed-point obstruction</summary>
In a Boolean algebra, if $x^c=x$, then $x=x\wedge x=x\wedge x^c= ot$ and also $x=xee x=xee x^c=	op$. Thus bottom equals top. Coordinate-swapping support negation avoids this collapse because it is not Boolean complementation.
</details>

## Build the finite theory

Five axioms introduce the three paradoxes, ordinary truth, and a finite soundness certificate. The only rule introduces double negation. Explore its closure here.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

The complete support audit distinguishes the theorem gluts from the false and gap witnesses.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the soundness argument</summary>
Every axiom has positive support. If a premise has positive support, its double negation denotes the same sentence and therefore retains positive support. Structural induction over derivation trees proves that every theorem is designated. Since the false witness has no positive support, it cannot be a theorem.
</details>

## See non-explosion directly

A contradiction is local: choosing any paradox provides a theorem that is its own negation, while the false witness remains underivable.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

The negation graph shows fixed points and the ordinary truth/falsehood swap.

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the limits of the construction</summary>
The paradox names are abstract self-negating constants. A full account of the historical Liar, Berry, and Russell arguments would require quotation, substitution, descriptions, and restricted comprehension. The soundness certificate expresses a finite interpreted property and is not a claim that a strong arithmetical theory proves its own classical consistency.
</details>

